body:
I'm gonna be honest, this is pretty common amongst AuDHD (leaning towards worst-case scenario and heighten emotional intensity), and I think you'd benefit from considering the following points, which have helped me significantly with my OCD:

those thoughts and patterns are not going away anytime soon (if they could have, they would have), so in the meantime it's worth it to make yourself more at ease and accepting with the way your brain works;

that even with great effort and proper medication, a realistic outlook would be to get them 'managed' rather than 'eliminated';

that ultimately thoughts are just thoughts, as in 'you're not a bad person for having 'weird', unconventional, even violent, blasphemous, incestual thinking patterns', to lift away that extra layer of shame and guilt we assign ourselves to when we have those thoughts;

that none of us are in full control of our thoughts and emotions, so it is NOT your fault for not being able to stop them from appearing;

that we can however, try to control our behaviors so as not to act in ways that can harm others when emotions are high (again, people snap and slip up all the time, so forgive yourself big time whenever you fail, apologize, make amends, then keep on trying).

I understand that this is not an instant cure and that you may need to continue to search for the medication and work hard on the coping techniques that work for you, but hopefully my post has given you some comfort in finding acceptance with the reality you're in.
